</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The June 57K print sits 53K below the consensus and 72K below the revised May figure. The dashed red line marks the 110K consensus. April and May combined downward revisions of 74K mean the prior months overstated labour market strength.</p>

<h2>What This Means for the BoC and Canadian Portfolios</h2>

<p>The softer US payroll picture has a distinct Canadian read-through that clients are unlikely to have worked out themselves.</p>

<p>The Bank of Canada meets July 15. The market is pricing approximately 96% probability of a hold at 2.25%. A weak US jobs report that reduces Fed hike expectations does not obviously change the BoC''s calculus, because the BoC''s bind is domestic: core inflation at roughly 2.1% with headline at 3.2%, GDP barely positive, unemployment at 6.6%, and an energy shock that is unwinding as Hormuz flows recover. The BoC was already going to hold. A softer US labour market modestly reduces the risk that Canadian inflation imports further pressure from the US side. But the July 15 decision is, and has been, a purely domestic call.</p>

<p>Where the NFP miss does matter for Canadian portfolios is the currency. USD/CAD moved from 1.4219 to around 1.4172 on July 2 as the US dollar weakened post-data. A weaker USD/CAD means a stronger loonie relative to where it was. For clients with US equity exposure, an appreciating CAD erodes returns when those positions are measured in Canadian dollars. This is not a crisis, but it is a factor that clients with concentrated US positions deserve to understand before they interpret their July statement.</p>

<p>The gold sector is the other Canadian variable. TSX gold miners were among the top performers on July 3, with gold itself trading above $4,183. For clients with resource-heavy TSX exposure, this is a direct portfolio benefit from the same data print that is worrying them about their US equity allocation.</p>

<p>The Morningstar Mind the Gap study, which compares fund returns to investor returns and attributes the gap to timing decisions, consistently finds that the average investor loses 1.5 to 2.0 percentage points annually relative to the fund they are invested in. The gap is largest in the years immediately following mixed data releases like this one, when bifurcated market signals trigger bifurcated client behaviour.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who follow financial headlines woke to "US economy adds just 57,000 jobs" and have spent the morning triangulating that against whatever they remember about prior strong data. The emotional state is not panic but unease: a sense that the story that was reliable has shifted, without clarity about which direction it shifted in. Clients who hold gold or bonds feel vindicated and want permission to increase that exposure. Clients who hold US equities feel exposed and want permission to reduce it. Both groups are seeking confirmation of an action they have already decided on.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">"The jobs number yesterday was softer than expected, and I want to give you the accurate picture before you react to the headlines. The 57,000 was partly distorted by hospitality losing 61,000 jobs due to seasonal factors related to the World Cup, and a large chunk of the improvement in the unemployment rate came from people leaving the workforce rather than finding jobs. So the picture is mixed rather than clearly bad. The US economy is slowing from its early 2026 pace, but it is not signalling recession. What happened yesterday was that markets reduced their expectation of a Federal Reserve rate hike in September from about 64% to about 50%. That is a meaningful shift for bond prices and gold, which is why you saw both rise. For your Canadian portfolio, the Bank of Canada meeting is July 15, and that decision remains a domestic call. The BoC''''s hands are tied by different pressures than the Fed''''s. Here is what I think is the right way to think about this for your situation."</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated US equity exposure who have been relying on a "strong US economy" narrative. That narrative is now less clean, and they need a reset of expectations. Clients approaching major spending events who are trying to time markets around the US data cycle.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with balanced equity and fixed income allocations. The bond portion has benefited from the yield decline; the equity portion has been less affected than the headlines suggest. The net effect for a balanced portfolio is roughly neutral to slightly positive.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients with Canadian gold sector or broad TSX exposure. TSX gold miners outperformed Thursday and Friday as gold moved above $4,183. Clients with CAD-denominated returns who were concerned about USD strength may find the CAD firming provides some relief.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review client portfolios with greater than 40% US equity exposure and identify which clients are most likely to call with rotation requests.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare a two-paragraph explanation of the participation rate mechanism for use in client conversations. Clients deserve to understand why the unemployment rate fell despite weak payrolls.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag clients who made portfolio changes after the May payroll miss or other recent data surprises. Loss aversion predicts they will want to act again. A proactive call before they reach out puts you in a better position.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document any client conversations where a client requests a portfolio change in response to the NFP data, including their stated rationale. This is useful context for future conversations about the cost of market timing.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review Canadian gold sector allocation for clients who may have reduced exposure during the June decline. The NFP-driven rally may create a re-entry discussion.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Friday morning context on the US jobs report<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
I wanted to give you a quick note on Thursday''''s US employment data before the long weekend, because the headlines do not fully capture what the report actually showed.<br><br>
The 57,000 jobs number was below consensus, but the shortfall was largely driven by leisure and hospitality losing 61,000 positions due to weaker seasonal hiring. The underlying sectors that have been driving growth, professional services, healthcare, and social assistance, continued to add jobs. The picture is one of moderation, not deterioration.<br><br>
The decline in the unemployment rate to 4.2% came partly from workers leaving the labour force rather than finding jobs. That is a less positive signal than the headline number suggests.<br><br>
For your portfolio, the main effect was a reduction in market expectations for a US Federal Reserve rate hike in September, which pushed bond prices higher and gold up sharply. The Bank of Canada''''s July 15 decision is driven by domestic Canadian conditions and remains largely unaffected by Thursday''''s US data.<br><br>
I will be in touch next week with a fuller picture heading into the BoC decision. As always, my recommendation is to hold your current allocation through this period rather than respond to a single data point.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''''respond-email'''', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors with US equity exposure:</strong> Thursday''''s data will have shaken people who built concentrated US equity positions on the "strong US economy" narrative. They have no advisor to call and no analytical framework for the participation rate detail. This is a high-probability prospecting window.</p>
<p><strong>Clients of advisors who went quiet:</strong> Any DIY investor or client of a less active advisor who did not receive a proactive communication after a data print this confusing is a warm prospect. The absence of guidance in an ambiguous moment is itself a prospecting signal.</p>
<p><strong>Clients holding cash who are waiting for clarity:</strong> Ambiguous data releases like this one convince cash-heavy investors that the fog will clear before they invest. In reality, ambiguity is the permanent condition of investing. This is a productive conversation to initiate.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">"I''''m calling because yesterday''''s US jobs number was more complicated than the headlines made it sound, and I thought you might appreciate a two-minute version of what it actually means for a Canadian investor."</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The investor who tries to trade around individual data releases is, on average, buying the wrong signal at the wrong time. Thursday''''s report was ambiguous enough that gold surged, equities went mixed, and the dollar weakened, all simultaneously. A market sending three different signals at once is not a market that rewards independent interpretation.</p>
<p>The Morningstar Mind the Gap study tracks the gap between what funds return and what investors actually receive. The gap exists almost entirely because of timing decisions made around events like Thursday''''s NFP. An advisor''''s job, in moments like this one, is to provide the analytical frame that prevents that gap from opening.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>When you saw the jobs number yesterday, what was your first instinct about your portfolio?</p>
<p>Have you made any changes to your investments in response to US economic data in the past year?</p>
<p>Do you have an advisor you called yesterday to talk through the implications?</p>
<p>How do you typically think about the relationship between US labour data and your Canadian investments?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The US jobs number and what it actually showed<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
I wanted to reach out after yesterday''''s US jobs report because the headline, 57,000 jobs and well below the 110,000 consensus, has been generating a lot of anxiety that may not be fully warranted.<br><br>
The shortfall was concentrated in hospitality, which lost 61,000 jobs due to unusual seasonal factors. The decline in the unemployment rate to 4.2% was partly driven by workers leaving the labour force rather than finding jobs, which is a less positive signal than the number suggests. The underlying data is one of moderation, not breakdown.<br><br>
For Canadian investors, the implications are specific and worth understanding: the Bank of Canada''''s July 15 decision is driven by domestic conditions and is not materially changed by this report. The gold sector on the TSX had a strong day as a direct result. And the US dollar weakened against the CAD, which has implications for portfolios with US exposure.<br><br>
If you would find it useful to talk through how Thursday''''s data affects your specific situation, I am happy to connect before the weekend is over.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''''prospect-email'''', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
