</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors with US equity in registered accounts:</strong> Most self-directed investors do not know the RRSP/TFSA withholding tax distinction. Many have US dividend payers sitting in TFSAs because TFSAs feel more accessible. This is a concrete, fixable problem that demonstrates immediate advisor value.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners with non-registered investment accounts:</strong> Incorporated professionals and business owners are more likely to hold US securities in non-registered corporate accounts, making them more exposed to the T1135 threshold and to CUSMA-related sector risk in some cases.</p>
<p><strong>Professionals with US employer equity:</strong> Engineers, finance professionals, and executives who receive US-listed stock through employer compensation plans often accumulate non-registered foreign property without a clear view of their T1135 position.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">"I''''m calling because the CUSMA review started this week, and there are two specific things in Canadian tax rules that connect to it that most investors with US holdings are not aware of. It takes about five minutes and could save you a meaningful amount each year."</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The RRSP/TFSA withholding tax distinction is a structural inefficiency that advisors fix and self-directed investors almost never discover on their own. At a 3% US dividend yield on a $200,000 position held in a TFSA instead of an RRSP, the annual cost is approximately $900 per year, compounding over decades. Over twenty years at 6% growth, the cost approaches $35,000. This is the kind of number that makes a prospect ask why their current advisor never mentioned it.</p>
<p>The T1135 story is simpler: the CRA requires the form, the penalty for missing it is automatic, and currency movements in 2026 have quietly pushed some investors past the threshold. Most people do not know they may be at risk.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Do you hold any US-listed investments inside your TFSA, such as US ETFs or individual US stocks?</p>
<p>Do you have US securities in non-registered accounts, and do you know the approximate Canadian dollar value?</p>
<p>Did your current advisor review your account placement when the RRSP/TFSA withholding tax rules last came up for you?</p>
<p>Are any of your investments connected to the auto sector, steel, or materials companies that would be directly affected by the CUSMA negotiation?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
