<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The GoC 5-year yield (solid line) has traded above the BoC policy rate (dashed line) throughout 2026, with the spread widening to 82 basis points at the July 2 reading of 3.07%. The shaded band marks the February-March Hormuz disruption onset.</p>

<h2>Why the MPR Matters More Than the Rate Decision</h2>

<p>The Monetary Policy Report, published concurrently with the July 15 rate decision, is where the analytical event actually occurs. The April MPR projected WTI at approximately $80 per barrel for Q2 and assumed the Hormuz disruption would ease gradually. WTI is now trading at roughly $68-69, nearly $12 below the April assumption. That gap has direct implications for the BoC''s headline inflation forecast: lower oil prices mechanically reduce gasoline price contributions to CPI, pulling headline inflation toward core faster than the April MPR expected.</p>

<p>The July MPR will therefore need to revise the headline inflation path downward from the April projection. Core inflation is less affected. The BoC''s trimmed mean and median measures have been anchored near 2.1%, and the energy price pass-through to core has been limited. The July MPR is likely to characterise this as the expected pattern: a temporary energy-driven headline spike that is now unwinding, with core remaining inside the band.</p>

<p>The growth forecast is the more uncertain revision. Q1 GDP came in at negative 0.1%, weaker than the April MPR assumed. Q2 data is incomplete but the BoC''s June 10 statement suggested growth is resuming. The May employment surprise, 88,000 jobs against a 10,000 consensus, provided a strong counterpoint to Q1 weakness. The July MPR will need to reconcile those two readings into a coherent growth trajectory for H2 2026.</p>

<p>What advisors should be watching on July 15 is not the rate decision itself but how Governor Macklem characterises the Hormuz unwinding''s effect on the inflation outlook. If the MPR''s revised path shows headline CPI returning to 2% by Q1 2027, the December hike scenario becomes considerably less likely. If it shows headline remaining above 2.5% through Q4 despite lower oil, core inflation risks have broadened and the conversation changes.</p>

<h2>The Bond Market Is Already Telling You the Answer</h2>

<p>The GoC 5-year yield at 3.07% on July 2 reflects the bond market''s current best estimate of the rate path. At 2.25% policy rate and 3.07% five-year yield, the spread is 82 basis points. That spread has two interpretations. The optimistic read is that the market expects the BoC to hold for an extended period, with the term premium reflecting duration risk rather than anticipated hikes. The cautious read is that the market is pricing a modest probability of one hike in the next eighteen months, most likely in December 2026 if core inflation doesn''t co-operate.</p>

<p>For clients renewing five-year fixed mortgages, the GoC 5Y yield is the primary input into their renewal rate. At 3.07% plus a typical lender spread of 1.0% to 1.15%, the all-in five-year fixed rate is approximately 4.07% to 4.22%. That is the number that matters for the 900,000 Canadian households estimated to be renewing fixed mortgages before year-end. The July 15 rate decision does not change that number. A meaningful change in GoC 5Y yields would.</p>

<p>Thursday''s US NFP miss created a modest downward pull on GoC yields through the integrated bond market linkage. US Treasury yields fell approximately 2 basis points to 4.46%. GoC yields tend to follow, with some lag and some divergence reflecting domestic factors. The directional effect is real but small. The mortgage renewal math does not materially change on the basis of a 2 to 5 basis point GoC yield movement driven by a US employment miss.</p>

<h2>What Advisors Should Tell Clients About July 15</h2>

<p>The Bank of Canada holds at 2.25% on July 15. The Monetary Policy Report will show lower near-term headline inflation than the April MPR projected, driven by the oil price correction. Core inflation remains anchored and inside the target band. The growth outlook for H2 2026 will be revised, most likely modestly upward from the weak Q1 reading, with the May employment data providing support. The rate path through the rest of 2026 remains conditional on whether core inflation holds near 2.1% or begins to drift.</p>

<p>The NFP miss makes the global growth environment somewhat softer, which slightly reduces the external inflation pressure on Canada, and modestly strengthens the case for BoC independence in holding rather than following the Fed toward any future hike. Neither of those effects is large enough to change the fundamental picture the BoC will present on July 15.</p>

<p>For clients watching the BoC announcement, the summary is straightforward: hold, as expected, with an updated MPR that will be worth reading carefully for what it says about the Hormuz-to-inflation transmission. The decision itself will contain no surprises.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who follow BoC news are often watching the rate path through the lens of their mortgage or their fixed income holdings. Thursday''''s US data has created a new layer of confusion: some clients will assume that a weaker US jobs report automatically means lower rates everywhere, including Canada. Others will be watching for signs that the BoC is being forced to choose between holding and hiking, and will want to know which way July 15 resolves. Most clients want certainty that does not exist. The correct response is to provide precision about what July 15 will and will not tell us.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">"The Bank of Canada is going to hold at 2.25% on July 15. That part is not in doubt. What is worth watching is the Monetary Policy Report they release at the same time, because it will update their oil price assumptions and their inflation forecast. Oil has come down from its peak by about $28 a barrel, and that will pull their headline inflation numbers down considerably. The question they are still working through is whether core inflation, which has been behaving well near 2.1%, stays that way. If it does, the rate hold probably continues through the end of the year. If it starts to drift up, the December meeting becomes a potential hike. But right now, the base case is a hold through 2026. Thursday''''s US jobs number does not change that. Canada''''s rate path is set by Canadian data."</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients renewing five-year fixed mortgages in H2 2026. The GoC 5Y at 3.07% translates to all-in fixed rates of approximately 4.07% to 4.22%. July 15 holds rather than cuts means no immediate relief for these clients.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients holding Canadian bond funds or fixed income ETFs. The July 15 hold is already priced in. The MPR''''s revised inflation path may produce a modest bond market reaction if it is more or less dovish than expected.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Variable rate mortgage holders. If the BoC is on hold through year-end, variable rates stay at prime minus their spread. The base case of no hike means no immediate payment increase for variable rate clients who feared a summer surprise.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients renewing fixed mortgages between July and December 2026 and confirm they have a renewal strategy that does not depend on a rate cut that is not coming.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review the MPR on July 15 for the revised oil price assumption and the revised headline CPI path. These are the two numbers that will set the narrative for the rest of 2026.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For clients with significant GoC bond or bond fund exposure, note that the July 15 hold is priced in. The MPR''''s language about December is what would move yields.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare a one-paragraph post-July 15 client note summarizing the BoC decision and the MPR''''s key revisions. This is a high-value touchpoint given the level of client attention on rates.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Bank of Canada July 15: what to expect and what actually matters<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
With the Bank of Canada meeting on July 15, I wanted to give you a clear picture of what to expect before the announcement.<br><br>
The rate decision itself will be a hold at 2.25%. The market is pricing approximately 96% probability of no change, and the domestic data does not support either a cut or a hike at this point. Canada''''s unemployment rate at 6.6% argues against a hike. Core inflation near 2.1% argues against a cut. The Bank is in a genuine hold position.<br><br>
What is worth watching is the Monetary Policy Report released alongside the decision. It will update the BoC''''s oil price assumptions, reflecting WTI falling from near $97 in May to roughly $68-69 now. That will pull their near-term headline inflation forecast lower. What it says about core inflation and the H2 2026 growth outlook will tell us whether December becomes a live meeting for a potential hike.<br><br>
Thursday''''s weak US jobs report does not change the July 15 picture. Canada''''s rate path is set by Canadian data, and none of those inputs have moved materially since the June 10 hold decision.<br><br>
I will send a brief note after the July 15 announcement with the key points from the MPR.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''''respond-email'''', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Clients renewing mortgages who are waiting for a rate cut:</strong> Many Canadian homeowners believe the BoC will cut before their renewal date. The base case through 2026 is a hold, not a cut. A proactive conversation with these clients now, before their renewal, is considerably more valuable than one after they have signed at a rate they did not anticipate.</p>
<p><strong>DIY investors who follow US rate news and assume it applies to Canada:</strong> Thursday''''s NFP miss generated significant coverage about the Fed''''s September rate hike probability. Investors who follow US financial media will be drawing incorrect conclusions about the BoC. This is a specific knowledge gap that an advisor closes in a single conversation.</p>
<p><strong>Clients in GIC ladders or bond funds asking about rate direction:</strong> Fixed income investors want to know whether to extend duration. The answer in a 2.25% hold environment with a modest December hike tail risk is nuanced. This is precisely the kind of conversation where advisor value is demonstrable and quantifiable.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">"I''''m reaching out because the Bank of Canada meets July 15, and there is a common misreading of what Thursday''''s US jobs data means for the Canadian rate path. The two central banks are actually on very different tracks right now, and it matters for how you plan around your fixed income and any upcoming mortgage renewal."</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The Fed and the BoC are facing structurally different economic conditions. The US is managing above-target inflation in an economy that was adding strong jobs through May. Canada is managing excess supply, a soft labour market, and an energy-driven headline spike that is now unwinding. These two situations call for different policy paths, and Canadian investors who follow US financial media are systematically misreading their own central bank''''s likely behaviour.</p>
<p>An advisor who explains this distinction clearly, before the July 15 announcement confirms it, demonstrates exactly the kind of forward-looking analytical value that separates professional advice from self-directed investing.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Do you have a fixed mortgage renewing in the next twelve months, and what rate are you planning for?</p>
<p>When you saw the US jobs report Thursday, did you assume that meant Canadian rates might also fall?</p>
<p>How are you currently positioned in fixed income: GICs, bond funds, or something else?</p>
<p>Do you have an advisor who walked you through the difference between the Fed''''s rate path and the BoC''''s right now?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Before the Bank of Canada meets July 15<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
The Bank of Canada announces its rate decision on July 15, along with an updated Monetary Policy Report. I wanted to reach out before that date with a clear picture of what to expect, because Thursday''''s US jobs data has created some confusion about what it means for Canada.<br><br>
The short answer: Canada''''s rate path is set by Canadian data, not US data. The Fed and the BoC are managing very different economic situations right now. The Bank of Canada will hold at 2.25% on July 15, as expected before Thursday''''s US report, and the domestic data that would drive a change in that stance has not materially shifted.<br><br>
What does matter is the Monetary Policy Report released alongside the decision. It will update the BoC''''s oil price assumptions and inflation path, reflecting WTI''''s fall from near $97 to roughly $68-69. How the Bank characterises the Hormuz unwinding''''s effect on the inflation outlook will set the agenda for the rest of 2026.<br><br>
If you have a mortgage renewal coming up, or fixed income holdings where the rate direction matters, I would be happy to talk through the implications before the announcement.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
