<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">WTI peaked near $96 in April 2026 as the Hormuz disruption reached its most acute phase, then fell 29% to approximately $68.56 as Hormuz flows recovered following the June 17 MOU. The green dashed line marks the pre-war reference price near $68.</p>

<h2>The Toll Regime Tail Risk</h2>

<p>The most analytically significant development from the Doha talks is the Iranian position on tanker tolls. Iranian officials have stated that they intend to impose transit fees on vessels using the Strait of Hormuz beginning in mid-August, framed as compensation for the management of a busy international waterway. Oman has separately outlined a proposed fee structure, pointing to the Strait of Malacca and Singapore as a possible precedent.</p>

<p>The US position is categorical: the Strait of Hormuz is an international waterway governed by the UN Convention on the Law of the Sea, and Iran has no legal basis to impose fees. US Vice President Vance confirmed in his CNN interview that the nuclear issue and Hormuz administration are both on the table in the next round of talks.</p>

<p>A functioning toll regime at Hormuz would represent a structural supply disruption rather than a military one. The economic effect would depend on the toll level and enforcement mechanism, but even a modest fee adds to the landed cost of Middle Eastern crude in Asia and Europe, supports a floor on global oil prices, and keeps insurance premiums elevated. For Canadian oil producers, a Hormuz toll floor on global prices would be incrementally positive, reducing the probability of a full oil price normalisation back to pre-war levels.</p>

<p>The probability of a functioning toll regime is currently low. The US has the military and diplomatic leverage to prevent it, and Iran''s negotiating position on tolls may be a bargaining chip rather than a firm policy intention. But the tail is non-trivial, and the July 10-onward negotiating round will be the first real test of whether Iran''s toll position is tactical or structural.</p>

<h2>Canadian Energy Sector: Asymmetric Exposure</h2>

<p>The TSX energy sub-index has repriced materially since its June 17 ATH-adjacent peak, tracking the oil price decline. Canadian integrated producers and heavy oil producers carry breakeven costs in the range of $40-55 WTI depending on the operation. At current prices near $68-69, the sector is profitable and generating strong free cash flow. The question for Canadian advisors is what the sector looks like under each resolution scenario.</p>

<p>Full Hormuz resolution and oil returning to the $60-65 range: Canadian producers remain profitable but the sector''s premium rerate from the energy shock unwinds. Energy names on the TSX would trade closer to their pre-conflict multiples. Dividend sustainability is not threatened at $60 WTI for most major Canadian producers, but the growth narrative weakens.</p>

<p>Partial resolution with a toll regime or continued Hormuz friction: oil floors near $68-75, and Canadian heavy oil producers benefit from the continued spread over breakeven. The sector maintains its elevated free cash flow profile and dividend coverage remains strong.</p>

<p>Re-escalation during or after the Khamenei funeral: the June 17 MOU contains a clause requiring both parties to negotiate for a maximum of 60 days, extendable by mutual consent. If the funeral creates a provocation, the most likely response is a pause in negotiations rather than a military reversal. But a military exchange during the funeral period would push oil back toward the $80-90 range rapidly.</p>

<p>For most diversified Canadian portfolios, the current base case of gradual Hormuz normalisation is already largely priced. The residual portfolio question is how much energy sector exposure is appropriate heading into a negotiating round that will determine whether the toll tail risk materialises.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who have been following the Hormuz story through the spring are experiencing a form of geopolitical fatigue. The situation has evolved through phases, from the initial war onset in February through the oil spike, the June 17 MOU, and now the Doha talks, without a clean resolution. Clients who held through the full cycle have seen their energy positions rise and then partially retrace. Some feel they should have sold at the peak. Others wonder if the situation is genuinely close to resolution. The dominant emotion is uncertainty about whether to hold, reduce, or add to energy exposure.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">"The talks in Doha produced what Qatar called positive progress, but no breakthrough. The next round is after the Khamenei funeral ends July 9, so for the next week or so the situation is in a holding pattern. Oil is near $68-69, which is essentially back to pre-war levels, and the market is treating the Hormuz situation as largely resolved at the flow level. The unresolved piece is Iran''''s stated intention to impose tanker tolls in mid-August. That is not in the market price right now, and if it becomes a real dispute in the next round of talks it would put a floor under oil. For your energy holdings, the current price is still well above most Canadian producers'''' breakeven costs, so the sector is profitable either way. The question is whether you want exposure to the upside if talks stall, or whether you would rather reduce ahead of what could be a period of lower volatility and lower oil."</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated TSX energy sector exposure who are trying to decide whether the trade is over. The asymmetric scenario analysis is the relevant framework: downside if oil normalises to $60-65, upside if toll regime creates a floor, and tail risk if funeral-period escalation pushes oil back toward $80-90.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with diversified TSX exposure that includes a standard energy weighting. The energy sector repricing from peak to current has already occurred. The residual question is sizing rather than direction.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients who reduced energy exposure ahead of the June 17 MOU and are now watching for a re-entry level. Current prices near pre-war levels represent a fundamentally different risk-reward than the peak.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review the energy sector weighting in client portfolios against their stated risk tolerance for geopolitical uncertainty. The holding pattern through July 9 is a natural review point.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag the mid-August toll regime deadline as a calendar event for client communications. If talks resume after the funeral and the toll issue is unresolved by late July, a proactive client note before August is warranted.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">For clients holding individual energy names rather than sector ETFs, review each company''''s stated WTI breakeven and free cash flow guidance to confirm dividend sustainability at current prices.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Note the ITF Warlike Operations Area designation expires July 9. If it is extended after the funeral, it signals elevated crew risk assessment and will likely add a modest insurance premium re-escalation to tanker costs.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Hormuz update: where the situation stands heading into the Khamenei funeral<br><br>
